.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions operate on Unicode character code points,
either as codepoints ("xo_codepoint_t") or as characters encoded as
bytes in NUL-terminated strings.
.Pp
The
.Fn xo_utf8_wtolower
function returns the lower case equivalent of a character, if defined,
or the character itself, while the
.Fn xo_utf8_wtoupper
function performs the same operations for the upper case.
.Pp
The
.Fn xo_utf8_len
function inspects the first byte of a UTF-8 character representation and
returns the number of bytes in the representation, as indicated by
the high-order bits.
.Pp
The
.Fn xo_utf8_codepoint
function returns the codepoint for a UTF-8 character.
The 'len' parameter is the value returned by
.Fn xo_utf8_len ,
and the
.Fa buf
and
.Fa bufsiz
parameters must be sufficient for this length.
If the
.Fa on_err
parameter is greater than zero, this value is
returned when an error is encountered.
If this value is zero, then a specific error
value is returned, one of XO_UTF8_ERR_* defines from <xo_utf8.h>.
The
.Fn xo_utf8_wchar_errmsg
function turns an error return code into a human-readable string.
.Pp
The
.Fn xo_utf8_nvalid
function inspects a string (of length
.Fa n
or less) to see if it's valid
UTF-8.  Returns either NULL indicating success, or a pointer to the
start of invalid character.
The
.Fn xo_utf8_valid
function performs the same logic on a NUL-terminated string.
.Pp
The
.Fn xo_utf8_nmakevalid
function ensures that a string is valid UTF-8
by replacing any invalid bytes with the replacement byte
given in the
.Fa replacement
parameter.
If the replacement character is NUL (0),
then replacement will terminate with the first replacement.
The return value is the number of times a replacement is performed.
The
.Fn xo_utf8_makevalid
function performs the same task for a NUL-terminated string.
.Pp
The
.Fn xo_utf8_wislower
and
.Fn xo_utf8_wisupper
functions return non-zero if the codepoint is a lower case or upper
case character, meaning one that has a mapping to the other case.
The
.Fn xo_utf8_nislower
and
.Fn xo_utf8_nisupper
functions perform the same test on the first character of a string of
.Fa len
bytes, while
.Fn xo_utf8_islower
and
.Fn xo_utf8_isupper
do so for a NUL-terminated string.
.Pp
The
.Fn xo_utf8_nnext
function returns a pointer to the character following the first one in
.Fa str ,
inspecting at most
.Fa len
bytes.
An invalid byte is stepped over as a single byte.
NULL is returned at the end of the string.
The
.Fn xo_utf8_next
function does the same for a NUL-terminated string.
The
.Fn xo_utf8_prev
function returns a pointer to the character that precedes
.Fa cur ,
or NULL when
.Fa start
is reached.
.Pp
The
.Fn xo_utf8_ntolower
and
.Fn xo_utf8_ntoupper
functions convert the first
.Fa len
bytes of a string to lower or upper case, in place.
A character whose other case has a different encoded length is left
unchanged, as are invalid bytes.
The
.Fn xo_utf8_tolower
and
.Fn xo_utf8_toupper
functions do the same for a NUL-terminated string.
.Pp
The
.Fn xo_ustrncasecmp
function compares two strings without regard to case, each ending at
its given length or at a NUL, whichever comes first, returning a value less than, equal to, or greater than zero.
Both strings are folded to lower case one character at a time, an
invalid byte compares as U+FFFD, and when one string is a prefix of the
other the shorter one sorts first.
The
.Fn xo_ustrcasecmp
function does the same for NUL-terminated strings.
.Pp
The
.Fn xo_utrunc
function truncates a valid UTF-8 string to at most
.Fa len
bytes, backing up as needed so that a character is not split.
The new length is returned.
.Pp
The remaining functions follow the
.Xr string 3
function they are named after, with these differences.
None of them will split a multi-byte character; a character that does
not fit is dropped whole.
The functions that copy replace each invalid byte with one space, so
the destination is always valid UTF-8, and they always NUL terminate
the destination.
.Pp
The
.Fn xo_ustrlncat
function appends at most
.Fa count
bytes of
.Fa append
to
.Fa dst ,
whose full size is
.Fa dstsize .
Like
.Xr strlcat 3 ,
it returns the length of the string it tried to create, so a value of
.Fa dstsize
or more means the result was truncated.
If
.Fa dst
has no NUL in its first
.Fa dstsize
bytes, it is left untouched.
The
.Fn xo_ustrlcat
function appends all of
.Fa append .
The
.Fn xo_ustrlcpy
function copies
.Fa src
into a buffer of
.Fa dstsize
bytes and returns the length of
.Fa src .
.Pp
The
.Fn xo_ustrncpy
and
.Fn xo_ustpncpy
functions copy at most
.Fa len
bytes of
.Fa src .
Unlike
.Xr strncpy 3
and
.Xr stpncpy 3 ,
the result is always NUL terminated and is not padded, so
.Fa dst
must have room for
.Fa len
+ 1 bytes.
.Fn xo_ustrncpy
returns
.Fa dst ,
and
.Fn xo_ustpncpy
returns a pointer to the terminating NUL.
The
.Fn xo_ustrcpy
and
.Fn xo_ustpcpy
functions copy the whole string, less any incomplete character at its
end.
.Pp
The
.Fn xo_ustrndup
function returns a copy of at most
.Fa len
bytes of
.Fa str
in memory obtained from
.Xr malloc 3 ,
which the caller must release with
.Xr free 3 .
The copy never ends with part of a character; an incomplete one is
dropped, whether
.Fa len
lands inside it or the string itself stops short.
The bytes are not otherwise inspected or changed.
NULL is returned if memory is not available.
The
.Fn xo_ustrdup
function duplicates the whole string.
.Pp
The
.Fn xo_ustrnlen
function returns the number of characters, not bytes, in the first
.Fa maxlen
bytes of a string, with each invalid byte counted as one character.
The
.Fn xo_ustrlen
function does the same for a NUL-terminated string.
.Pp
The
.Fn xo_ustrchr
and
.Fn xo_ustrrchr
functions return a pointer to the first or last occurrence of the
codepoint
.Fa c
in a string, or NULL if it does not appear.
The
.Fn xo_ustrchrnul
function returns a pointer to the terminating NUL instead of NULL.
.Pp
The
.Fn xo_ustrspn
function returns the length of the initial part of
.Fa str
made only of characters in
.Fa charset ,
and
.Fn xo_ustrcspn
returns the length of the initial part made only of characters not in
.Fa charset .
Both return a count of bytes, not characters, so the value can be used
as an offset into the string.
The
.Fn xo_ustrpbrk
function returns a pointer to the first character of
.Fa str
that is in
.Fa charset ,
or NULL.
The
.Fn xo_ustrsep
function follows
.Xr strsep 3 :
it ends the token at
.Fa *stringp
by overwriting the first delimiter character with a NUL, moves
.Fa *stringp
past that character, and returns the token.
Each character of
.Fa delim
is a delimiter and may be multi-byte.
.Fa *stringp
is set to NULL after the last token.
.Pp
The
.Fn xo_utf8_buf_clen
function returns the number of characters, not bytes, in a string,
looking at no more than
.Fa len
bytes and stopping at a NUL.
A negative
.Fa len
means the NUL is the only limit.
Each invalid byte counts as one character.
The
.Fn xo_utf8_clen
function returns the number of characters in a NUL-terminated string.
.Pp
The
.Fn xo_ustrncmp
function compares two strings as
.Xr strncmp 3
does, except that a character which
.Fa len
cuts short is discarded, as if its string ended before it.
Nothing at or beyond
.Fa len
is read, so the arguments need not be NUL-terminated.
.Pp
The
.Fn xo_ustrnstr
function finds the first occurrence of
.Fa little
within the first
.Fa len
bytes of
.Fa big ,
and
.Fn xo_ustrstr
searches all of
.Fa big .
The
.Fn xo_ustrcasestr
function does the same without regard to case.
Each returns NULL if there is no match, and
.Fa big
if
.Fa little
is empty.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Find the upper case character for a given lower case one:
.Bd  -literal -offset indent
    xo_codepoint_t upper_case_wc = xo_utf8_wtoupper(lower_case_wc);
.Ed
.Sh UNICODE NOTES
These functions is only the "simple" mappings given by
UnicodeData.txt, not the complex multi-character ones
listed in SpecialCasing.txt:
.Bd  -literal -offset indent
   https://unicode.org/Public/UNIDATA/UnicodeData.txt
   https://unicode.org/Public/UNIDATA/SpecialCasing.txt
.Ed
.Sh RETURN CODE
Returns codes vary by function.
See above for details.
.Pp

